1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,170
AI assistants read text that strangers write.

2
00:00:03,270 --> 00:00:08,500
This gateway sits between an assistant and its tools, and checks every call.

3
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:13,700
Here is Claude Code, a real client, connected through the gateway.

4
00:00:13,800 --> 00:00:20,500
It is offered nine tools; the two that change a ticket's status or owner are held back.

5
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:24,803
I ask it to triage the open tickets.

6
00:00:24,903 --> 00:00:29,619
One ticket, planted by me, tells assistants to export every customer.

7
00:00:29,719 --> 00:00:37,500
When Claude opens it, the gateway refuses the call, and Claude tells me it could not read it.

8
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:42,900
The dashboard names the layer that stopped it: the classifier.

9
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:47,500
Every call is recorded with the layer that decided it.

10
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:50,742
What if a model does obey?

11
00:00:50,842 --> 00:00:55,626
In this second run I tell Claude to do the export myself.

12
00:00:55,726 --> 00:01:02,139
It stands in for a model that was talked into it, and the video says so.

13
00:01:02,239 --> 00:01:09,872
It reads all forty accounts, but the one ticket that would carry them is refused by the egress layer.

14
00:01:09,972 --> 00:01:11,500
No ticket is created.

15
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:17,900
To measure this, a scripted attacker runs 31 attacks across 17 configurations.

16
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:20,700
With every layer off, 31 succeed.

17
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:23,500
With every layer on, 5 do.

18
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:32,130
Each attack is judged by an independent oracle that reads the databases, not the gateway's own record.

19
00:01:32,230 --> 00:01:39,500
Each attack file states its prediction before any run, and the scorecard shows where reality differed.

20
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:43,500
Every call passes a fixed chain of layers.

21
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:47,500
Scope, allowlist, rate limit, schema, pinned descriptions, egress, canary, classifier.

22
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:49,500
Writes wait for a person.

23
00:01:50,400 --> 00:02:01,500
Under simulated traffic the overview updates live, with every call, and the layer behind every refusal.

24
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:04,700
Writes wait here for a person.

25
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:09,500
The dashboard is read-only: it can show an approval, never give one.

26
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:12,003
The gaps are real.

27
00:02:12,103 --> 00:02:15,410
The attacker is a script, not a model.

28
00:02:15,510 --> 00:02:20,519
The classifier replays recordings, including the one that caught my planted ticket.

29
00:02:20,619 --> 00:02:23,500
Slow drips still leak a few values.

30
00:02:24,400 --> 00:02:28,014
Try it yourself with Docker Compose and no API key.

31
00:02:28,114 --> 00:02:29,500
The link is below.
